WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.800
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.930
We have talked about fluid and cloth simulations
earlier, but we never really set foot in the

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.750
domain of hair simulations in the series.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:18.610
To obtain some footage of virtual hair movement,
simulating the dynamics of hundreds of thousands

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:24.029
of hair strands is clearly too time consuming
and would be a flippant attempt to do so.

00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:29.480
If we do not wish to watch a simulation unfold
with increasing dismay as it would take hours

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:35.039
of computation time to obtain just one second
of footage, we have to come up with a cunning

00:00:35.039 --> 00:00:36.039
plan.

00:00:36.039 --> 00:00:41.920
A popular method to obtain detailed real-time
hair simulations is not to compute the trajectory

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:48.729
of every single hair strand, but to have a
small set of strands that we call guide hairs.

00:00:48.729 --> 00:00:51.550
For these guide hairs, we compute everything.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:57.010
However, since this is a sparse set of elements,
we have to fill the gaps with a large number

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:03.119
of hair strands, and we essentially try to
guess how these should move based on the behavior

00:01:03.119 --> 00:01:05.020
of guide hairs near them.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:10.400
Essentially, one guide hair is responsible
in guiding an entire batch, or an entire braid

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.110
of hair, if you will.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:17.260
This technique we like to call a reduced hair
simulation, and the guessing part is often

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.810
referred to as interpolation.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:25.450
And the question immediately arises: how many
guide hairs do we use and how many total hair

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:31.000
strands can we simulate with them without
our customers finding out that we're essentially

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.000
cheating?

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.340
The selling point of this piece of work, is
that it uses only 400 guide hairs, and leaning

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:45.620
on them, and it can simulate up to a total
number of 150 thousand strands in real time.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.170
This leads to amazingly detailed hair simulations.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:53.400
My goodness, look at how beautiful these results
are!

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:58.840
Not only that, but as it is demonstrated quite
aptly here, it can also faithfully handle

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:02.810
rich interactions and collisions with other
solid objects.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:07.620
For instance, we can simulate all kinds of
combing, or pulling our hair out, which is

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:13.340
what most researchers do in their moments
of great peril just before finding the solution

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:14.879
to a difficult problem.

00:02:14.879 --> 00:02:20.099
Not only hair, but a researcher simulator,
if you will.

00:02:20.099 --> 00:02:26.739
The results are compared to a full space simulation,
which means simulating every single hair strand,

00:02:26.739 --> 00:02:30.299
and that is exactly as time consuming as it
sounds.

00:02:30.299 --> 00:02:35.769
The results are very close to being indistinguishable,
which was not the case for previous works

00:02:35.769 --> 00:02:41.249
that created false intersections where hair
strands would erroneously go through solid

00:02:41.249 --> 00:02:42.249
objects.

00:02:42.249 --> 00:02:47.580
We can also stroke bunnies with our hair models
in this truly amazing piece of work.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:52.080
These episodes are also available in early
access for our Patreon supporters.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.420
We also have plenty of other really neat perks,
I've put a link in the description box, make

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:57.529
sure to have a look!

00:02:57.529 --> 00:03:02.009
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

